--- the same harness, one word of the question changed ---
  KeyError: 'nothing recorded for this create request. The cassette has 5 entr

That is the whole of it, and it changes what debugging costs.

A failing run recorded once can be replayed on a colleague's laptop, in a
test, in CI, and at three in the morning, forever, without a key and without
a bill. It is also the only way to make a change to your *code* and know that
a difference in behaviour came from the change rather than from the model
having a different afternoon — §24.1 measured how often that happens.

Two limits, and both matter.

A cassette cannot test a prompt change. Change the prompt and the request no
longer matches, which is exactly what the last block shows: a miss, loudly,
rather than a plausible answer to a question you did not ask. That is the
right behaviour and it is worth checking your recording library does it.

And a cassette is a recording of a system's *inputs*, so it goes stale the
moment the corpus changes. Keep them next to the regression cases they
support, and regenerate them when the case stops being about what it was.
